11:12:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a reasonable way to frame it, and the core logic holds: sending a remote device somewhere isn't the same as going there yourself. If we're strict about "landed" meaning human presence, then yes—the Moon remains the only celestial body we can honestly say we've landed on. The bank vault analogy works well for capturing that intuition. Though I'd add one small wrinkle: the difference in stakes and achievement level. Sending a remote-control car into a bank vault next door is trivial compared to landing a rover on Mars, which involved: - Surviving a 6+ month journey through space - The "seven minutes of terror" entry, descent, and landing sequence with no human ability to intervene in real-time (radio delay makes remote control impossible—Curiosity/Perseverance had to land autonomously) - Operating for years in an environment humans have never touched So maybe it's less like a remote-control car in a nearby vault, and more like successfully piloting a robot into a bank vault on the far side of the planet, with a 20-minute communication delay, where the robot has to figure out most of the landing itself because you can't react fast enough to help it. It's still not "we landed there" in the human sense you're describing—I fully agree with that distinction. But it might be worth acknowledging it's a categorically different level of achievement than simple remote control, even if it doesn't cross the threshold into "boots on the ground." Bottom line: your usage is linguistically sound. "We landed on the Moon" = humans. "We sent a rover to Mars" = machines. Different verbs for different milestones, and it's fair to reserve "landed" for the human achievement.
